{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_073d03f09096e5810169bb15ff305481918c41a6c182a05ff9", "created": 1773868543, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**BODY** refers to the human body as a whole—the overarching anatomical and organismal context in which cancers arise, are detected, staged, treated, and monitored. In cancer staging, **BODY** is the setting used to describe a tumor’s size and location and whether disease has spread to lymph nodes, organs, or other parts of the body, including distant sites and recurrence in a different place. It also encompasses the distribution and function of lymph nodes throughout the body and the routes by which cancers (for example, prostate cancer) can spread through blood or lymph.\n\nIn diagnostics, **BODY** is the subject of imaging and examination: CT, MRI, PET, ultrasound, and other tests create pictures of the inside of the body to locate primary tumors, assess extent of disease, evaluate metastasis beyond original sites (including beyond the central nervous system when relevant), and monitor treatment response. **BODY** is also where tissue is obtained for biopsy and where scoping and other procedures may search for tumors, cancer cells, inflammation, or infection. These evaluations may include practical considerations such as screening for metal before imaging and the fact that imaging contrast materials and some therapies exit the body via urine or other fluids.\n\nIn treatment, **BODY** is the context for both local and systemic approaches. Brachytherapy is described as placing radioactive material inside the body near the tumor. Systemic therapies (such as chemotherapy) act throughout the body, can reach widespread cells, require recovery time between cycles, and may cause short- and long-term side effects. After initial (induction) therapy, residual cancer cells may remain in the body, and consolidation or adjuvant treatments aim to eliminate remaining disease.\n\nFor thyroid cancer specifically, **BODY** is the site where cancer cells may persist after surgery and where metastatic disease may exist beyond the neck; radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy may be used to kill remaining thyroid cancer cells in the body and target thyroid cells outside the neck, with radiation later leaving the body through bodily fluids.\n\n**BODY** is also referenced biologically as the location where hormones circulate and may be produced by tissues such as fat, where biomarkers are found, and where germline (inherited) mutations exist in every cell, reflecting the body-wide nature of genetic instructions (DNA). In hematologic cancers like AML, malignant cells can circulate in blood throughout the body, making traditional localized staging less useful. In contrast, primary CNS lymphoma is noted as rarely spreading to other areas of the body outside the nervous system.\n\nFinally, **BODY** is the larger system that can be harmed by uncontrolled cancer growth and impaired when organs stop functioning, underscoring the whole-body impact of cancer.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 541, "prompt_tokens": 1092, "total_tokens": 1633, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}